React Hook Form Architecture
Install the library and understand its local form-state model

Phase 21 - Forms with React Hook Form + Zod
Volume 06 - Professional Frontend

Install from the web workspace
Run the packages from the frontend application, not from the monorepo root unless you intentionally
use a workspace filter.

# From: taskflow\apps\web
pnpm add react-hook-form@7.88.0 @hookform/resolvers@5.9.1

Minimal React Hook Form
"use client";

import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";

type Values = { title: string };

export function ExampleForm() {
const {
register,
handleSubmit,
formState: { errors, isSubmitting, isDirty },
} = useForm<Values>({
defaultValues: { title: "" },
});

return (
<form onSubmit={handleSubmit(async (values) => {
console.log(values);
})}>
<input {...register("title")} />
<button disabled={isSubmitting} type="submit">Save</button>
</form>
);
}

Important pieces
API                           Meaning

register                      Connect a native field to RHF

handleSubmit                  Validate/collect before invoking submit handler

formState.errors              Current field/form errors

isSubmitting                  True while the submit promise is pending

isDirty                       Current values differ from defaults

reset                         Replace defaults/current values and reset state

setError                      Attach client-visible errors, including server errors

Why a Client Component?
React Hook Form uses hooks and browser interaction, so the form component needs "use client". The
page that loads initial data can still remain a Server Component and pass serializable defaults into the
form.

Server Component loads Task / permissions

Pass serializable form defaults

Client TaskForm owns interaction

Submit to NestJS API

Figure 1 - Keep the client boundary around the interactive form, not around the entire route.

